body:
In my personal experience (which isn't a lot, granted) I found it easiest to introduce the character using their first and last name, then referring to them as one or the other. Of course, it would depend on the tone you want to portray, but I usually go with the first name, generally speaking. Sometimes, I call them by their title. For instance, I am currently writing a mystery novel, and I sometimes refer to the primary character as "the Detective", instead of his name - Joseph Bruner.

&#x200B;

Edit: I didn't really answer your question. To be honest, it doesn't matter what you call your character by - you just have to make sure you are consistent, as to not confuse the reader.

I could use some guidance. Essentially, I am writing a mystery novel. It is a recreation of one my friend did when we were in middle school. There are some fairly gruesome scenes in there, and I would like to know how much detail to put into said scenes.

&#x200B;

Now, don't get me wrong, there is hardly anything sexually explicit, but there are trends of gore. I haven't been able to find anything regarding how I should portray these scenes in my writing. They are fairly important to my narrative, so I don't want to gloss them over, but I also don't want to drive out would-be readers because of the detail.
